<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Jamaica</h1><p class="st-meta">1962 CE – present · capital Kingston</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The largest English-speaking island of the Caribbean, independent since 1962 and a parliamentary monarchy famed worldwide for reggae and athletics.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After Jamaicans voted to leave the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/west-indies-federation">West Indies Federation</a> in 1961, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-jamaica">Colony of Jamaica</a> became independent on 6 August 1962 as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commonwealth-of-nations">Commonwealth</a> realm, with the British monarch as head of state and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-bustamante">Alexander Bustamante</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica-labour-party">Jamaica Labour Party</a> as prime minister.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Politics has been a contest between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica-labour-party">Jamaica Labour Party</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-national-party">People's National Party</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-manley">Michael Manley's</a> democratic socialist government of 1972 to 1980 raised the bauxite levy and allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, while political violence culminated in about 800 deaths around the 1980 election, won by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-seaga">Edward Seaga</a>. Bauxite, tourism, remittances and agriculture are the main economic pillars. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rastafari">Rastafari</a> and reggae, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bob-marley">Bob Marley</a>, made Jamaican culture globally influential.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Jamaica remains a parliamentary democracy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>, although governments have pledged to become a republic. It has faced high debt and violent crime, while its athletes, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usain-bolt">Usain Bolt</a>, have dominated world sprinting.</p></div><aside class="st-side">